ds near 94 percent. Macy's fundraising advantage, Spanberger's appearances, and Democratic-tilted early voting justify a somewhat closer expected margin than the district's history alone would suggest.", "watch": "If Democratic-leaning early voting holds through Election Day the margin could tighten further, while a return to normal Election Day patterns would restore Cline's typical advantage.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Incumbent Ben Cline (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 6th District leans Republican by about 12 points, and Ben Cline is seeking another term as its incumbent. The only public poll, from a Republican-leaning firm in late September, showed him ahead 59 to 29 among likely voters, and prediction markets price his chances near 94 percent. No rating outlets list the race, and the district's history strongly favors the sitting party.\n\nStill, the contest has drawn unusual attention. Democrat Beth Macy has out-raised Cline and attracted national Democratic money, while Governor Spanberger has campaigned with her in the race's final weeks. Early voting numbers show a shift toward Democrats that, if sustained, could narrow the margin further. Even so, the district's lean and the incumbent's advantages make Cline a clear but not unassailable favorite, with a strong Democratic environment keeping the door slightly open.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0375, "margin": -14.4}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0454, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0463, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0654, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0527, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0505, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0614, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0306, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0328, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2